Subject: [PATCH 1/4] refactor: the jev stamp comes from its owner, not from a
 second derivation here
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Follow-on 4 of PR #41, applied on a scratch merge of this branch with chat
sync's asof-hold f61f5d4 (which carries #47). Not merged in-branch: their hold
is stacked on #47, so merging it into the #41 source would put two other pull
requests inside #41.

jevEvaluated() derived its own MAX(evaluated_at) over manager_archetype_jev.
archetypesBuilt(leagueId, season, memberId) in manager-archetypes.js owns that
stamp as jev_as_of, and two accessors deriving one store's stamp is the
duplication the as-of rule warns about: they drift, and then two surfaces date
one measurement differently. The answers are still read here — theirs returns a
stamp and a count, not the distributions — but the DATE now comes from the owner,
cached per member so a manager with eight answered questions is one read.

jev_as_of and jev_answers are OMITTED rather than nulled when no member is
passed, because "you did not ask about a member" and "no answers stored for him"
are different facts. So the presence test is `'jev_as_of' in built`, never a null
check, and this reads it that way.

The import is aliased to archetypeStoreStamps because this module exports an
archetypesBuilt of its own and the two are not the same function: theirs is the
member-keyed owner, mine the narrower league-season read the trade path already
uses. The collision is real and is named rather than hidden; folding my own into
their priced_as_of is follow-on 2 and is not in scope here.

season is threaded back through jevEvaluated, managerModelReads and the manager
page because their signature takes it. jev_as_of itself is not season-scoped —
manager_archetype_jev has no season column — so only the stamp lookup's sibling
fields depend on it, and this reads none of those.

Suites on this tree: valuation-map, manager-signals-api and their own
archetype-as-of, 93 tests, 93 pass, 0 fail.

